   "text": "http : / / usatoday30 . usatoday . com / news / world / story / 2011 - 11 - 10 / sudan - violence / 51150434 / 1 South Sudan <12 warns> of <14 war> after Sudan <13 bombs> refugee camp Military aircraft from Sudan <15 crossed> the new international border with South Sudan and <16 dropped> bombs Thursday in and around a camp <17 filled> with refugees , officials <18 said> . A government official initially <19 reported> <9 deaths> , but an American activist who <20 spoke> to aid workers at the camp later <21 said> there were no <53 casualties> . The <3 violence> in and near the Yida refugee camp , <27 located> 10 miles south of the border , <5 came> one day after <4 bombings> were <25 reported> in another region of South Sudan , an <6 attack> that <2 provoked> strong <7 condemnation> from the U . S . State Department . The president of South Sudan , which became the world's newest country only four months ago , said he fears the Khartoum - based government intends to invade the south soon . \"Whatever allegations Khartoum labels against the Republic of South Sudan are baseless , but intended to justify his pending invasion of the south , \" President Salva Kiir said . He later added : \"We are committed to peaceful resolutions to any conflict but we will never allow our sovereignty to be violated by anybody . \" There was no immediate comment from the Sudanese government in Khartoum on Thursday . The violence is especially troubling given the history between the two sides : The black African tribes of South Sudan and the mainly Arab north battled two civil wars over more than five decades , and some 2 million died in the latest war , from 1983 - 2005 . A peace deal ended the war and South Sudan became its own country in July after a successful independence referendum . But there have been lingering disputes over border demarcation and oil - sharing revenues . Ryan Boyette , a <26 former> aid worker who <8 lives> in Sudan who is now <11 leading> a team of 15 citizen journalists , <23 said> he <22 talked> to five aid workers in the Yida camp , all of whom <24 said> that four bombs were <10 dropped> but that they <1 caused> no <54 casualties> .",
